In web development, when you're using the Axum framework (a web application framework written in Rust), you might need to define a catch-all route. A catch-all route matches any URI not matched by previous routes, acting as a fall-back. It's useful for displaying 404 pages, for instance, or redirecting users to a specific part of your application.

As of my last update in April 2023, in Axum, to define a catch-all route, you can use a route that matches any path using a wildcard pattern. Here's an example to demonstrate how you might set this up:

```rust
use axum::{
    routing::get,
    Router,
    http::StatusCode,
    response::{IntoResponse, Response},
};
use std::net::SocketAddr;

async fn general_route() -> &'static str {
    "This matches specific paths"
}

async fn catch_all_route() -> impl IntoResponse {
    (StatusCode::NOT_FOUND, "This is a catch-all route, possibly a 404")
}

#[tokio::main]
async fn main() {
    // Define your app routes
    let app = Router::new()
        .route("/specific_path", get(general_route))
        // The catch-all route; it uses a wildcard pattern to match any path
        .route("/*catch_all", get(catch_all_route));

    // Define the address to run the server on
    let addr = SocketAddr::from(([127, 0, 0, 1], 3000));
    println!("Listening on http://{}", addr);

    // Run the server
    axum::Server::bind(&addr)
        .serve(app.into_make_service())
        .await
        .unwrap();
}
```

In this example:
- There's a specific route (`/specific_path`) handled by `general_route`.
- The `catch_all_route` is defined to handle any other paths that don't match previous routes, thus acting as a catch-all route. It's indicated by the `"/*catch_all"` pattern. Note that the part `catch_all` serves as a placeholder and doesn't specifically influence the catch-all behavior; it's the `*` (wildcard) that makes it a catch-all route. You could use any name you prefer.
- For any path other than `/specific_path`, the `catch_all_route` will be called, returning a 404 status code and a custom message.

Keep in mind that Axum and its syntax could evolve, so it's always a good idea to refer to the latest documentation for the most current features and best practices.

